---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="San Lee · Technical Program Manager, AI Systems"
  description="Technical program manager building reliable AI systems through clear direction, explicit contracts, and evidence-based delivery."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/"
  ogType="website"
  ogDescription="Technical program manager building reliable AI systems through clear direction, explicit contracts, and evidence-based delivery."
  skipTo="#work"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "San Lee",
    "url": "https://sanlee.me/",
    "jobTitle": "Senior Product Associate",
    "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "JPMorganChase" },
    "description": "Technical program manager building reliable AI systems through clear direction, explicit contracts, and evidence-based delivery.",
    "sameAs": [
      "https://github.com/sanlee-ys",
      "https://www.linkedin.com/in/leesan/"
    ]
  }
  </script>
  <style is:inline>
    /* Method plate (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: the three-part way
       the work is run. Not a second system diagram (that sits below).
       Max-width 260, viewBox 1:1, same grammar as .jtbd-strip. */
    .method-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .method-plate .mp-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .method-plate .mp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .method-plate .mp-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 8px;
    }
    .method-plate .mp-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .method-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .method-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0;
    }

    /* Flagship block (promoted above the router). Same rule-and-air rhythm as
       .orientation so the two read as siblings: a promoted entry, then the
       index. No accent fill and no card: the site's austerity is the
       credibility signal, and a banner here would argue against the page's own
       thesis. No opacity on text (ADR-009). */
    .flagship {
      margin: 52px 0 0;
      padding: 18px 0 4px;
      border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
    }
    .flagship .kicker { margin: 0 0 10px; }
    .flagship .section-title { margin-top: 0; }
    /* Counted figures, set as a record: mono, tabular, not a headline number. */
    .flagship-figure {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      margin: 14px 0 0;
    }
    .flagship-figure span { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <header class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>San Lee</h1>
      <p class="tagline">Technical program manager building reliable AI systems.</p>
      <p class="now">Product at JPMorganChase, on collaboration platforms serving 300,000 employees, after seven years in infrastructure and operations.</p>
      <p class="lede">
        I set direction, define the interfaces between teams and systems, and
        gate delivery on evidence. I use AI agents to accelerate execution while
        retaining ownership of the architecture, decisions, and quality bar.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <SiteNav />


  <div class="wrap proof-band">
    <p class="log-intro band-intro">Calls that shipped, and calls that were reversed.</p>
    <ul class="proof" aria-label="What the record shows">
      <li data-outcome="reversal"><a href="#decision-grounding"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span><span class="proof-claim">Added retrieval grounding, cut it</span> <strong>fixed 0, broke 4</strong></a></li>
      <li data-outcome="reversal"><a href="#decision-model"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span><span class="proof-claim">Built the premium-model upgrade, declined it</span> <strong>0 rows at 1.97&times; the cost</strong></a></li>
      <li><a href="#decision-eval"><span class="proof-claim">Rebuilt the eval until accuracy <em>fell</em></span> <strong>97.3% &rarr; 88.9%</strong></a></li>
      <!-- The one entry that resolves to a page rather than to a card below.
           The band is otherwise an index into the decision log, so this breaks
           that pattern deliberately: the claim is about the instruments rather
           than about one decision, and its proof is six incidents, which is a
           page and not a card. Kept last so the three classifier claims still
           read as a set. -->
      <li class="proof-span"><a href="projects/false-green.html"><span class="proof-claim">Found six of my own checks passing on work that never ran</span> <strong>green in 36s, nothing posted</strong></a></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mark-legend"><span class="mark-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>A doubled rule marks a decision that was reversed.</p>
  </div>

  <main class="wrap" id="work" tabindex="-1">
    <!-- Flagship, promoted above the router. Not a fifth door: the router
         holds four views of one system, and this is the instrument that
         measures the capacity that built them. Same rule-and-air grammar as
         .orientation below, so it reads as a promoted entry rather than a
         banner. Every figure here is marked data-tt and is gated by
         scripts/check-telltale-evidence.cjs. No number is typed by hand. -->
    <section class="flagship" aria-label="Flagship project">
      <p class="kicker">The instrument</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">telltale</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">Five coding-agent CLIs, one brief, five columns &mdash; and gauges that render nothing when nothing was measured.</p>
      <p>
        The agents that build this record are the thing I most needed to
        measure. telltale reads what five agent CLIs leave on disk and draws
        one row per seat. A reading no vendor exposes renders as an em dash,
        never as a zero, and <span data-tt="goldens.zeroVsAbsent">3</span>
        golden files in three packages fail the build if those two ever draw
        the same.
      </p>
      <p class="flagship-figure">v0.2.0 &middot; <span data-tt="goldens.total">132</span> golden files &middot; <span data-tt="tests.total">1780</span> test functions</p>
      <p class="diagram-more"><a class="card-link" href="projects/telltale.html">Read the telltale writeup <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="orientation" aria-label="What this record demonstrates">
      <p class="kicker">Selected work</p>
      <p class="orientation-lede">Four views of the work: how I structured the system, measured its decisions, tested a judge, and managed the program. Deeper investigations remain linked throughout the record.</p>
      <ul class="doors-index" aria-label="Four flagship case studies">
        <li><a href="projects/the-system.html"><span class="door-name">The System</span><span class="door-why">Architecture, interfaces, and the integration loop</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html"><span class="door-name">Classifier</span><span class="door-why">Evaluation, reversals, and evidence-based tradeoffs</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="projects/faithfulness-judge.html"><span class="door-name">Faithfulness Judge</span><span class="door-why">Can an LLM reliably catch a made-up claim?</span></a></li>
        <li><a href="projects/product-and-program.html"><span class="door-name">Product &amp; Program</span><span class="door-why">The work read through a TPM lens</span></a></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="diagram-more"><a class="card-link" href="projects/classifier-baseline.html#try-it">Run the classifier demo <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a></p>

      <!-- How the work is run. Open pipeline, no oxide: this is the method,
           not a reversal. ADR-010 N/A. -->
      <svg class="method-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
           aria-label="How the work is run: set the direction, freeze the contracts between the parts, then gate every change on evidence.">
        <title>Direction, then contracts, then an evidence gate.</title>
        <defs>
          <marker id="mp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <rect class="mp-box" x="2" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="mp-title" x="40" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Direction</text>
        <text class="mp-micro" x="40" y="38" text-anchor="middle">set the aim</text>
        <path class="mp-edge" d="M 78 26 L 90 26" marker-end="url(#mp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="mp-box" x="92" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="mp-title" x="130" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Contracts</text>
        <text class="mp-micro" x="130" y="38" text-anchor="middle">freeze seams</text>
        <path class="mp-edge" d="M 168 26 L 180 26" marker-end="url(#mp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="mp-box" x="182" y="6" width="76" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="mp-title" x="220" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Evidence</text>
        <text class="mp-micro" x="220" y="38" text-anchor="middle">gate the merge</text>
        <text class="mp-micro" x="130" y="64" text-anchor="middle">eval gates the merge</text>
      </svg>
    </section>

    <h2 class="section-title">The system</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">Three services wired into one loop: an API, an LLM classifier, and a RAG agent.</p>
    <figure class="diagram-wrap">
      <p class="flow-line" aria-label="System data flow, in order">
        Notes API <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        BackgroundTask <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Classifier <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Tags <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Knowledge base <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        KB Agent <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        Grounded answer
      </p>
      <svg class="diagram" id="system-diagram" viewBox="0 0 800 430" role="group" aria-label="Interactive map of the system: notes-api, classifier, and kb-agent, connected by an async BackgroundTasks enrichment loop. Select a component to see what it does and the decision behind it.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>
        <g id="diagram-edges"></g>
        <g id="diagram-nodes"></g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="diagram-detail" id="diagram-detail" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">Select a component to see what it does and the decision behind it.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="diagram-more"><a class="card-link" href="projects/the-system.html">Read the full writeup <span class="arrow">&rarr;</span></a></p>

    <section class="measure">
      <h2 class="section-title">The decision log</h2>

      <div class="decision" id="decision-grounding" data-outcome="reversal">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>BM25 retired<span class="code-ref">EVAL &middot; BM25</span></p>
        <h3>I added retrieval grounding, measured it, and cut it</h3>
        <p>I added BM25 lexical retrieval to ground each call, expecting a clear win. The first measurement said it barely helped. Then I found the measurement itself was unfair.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The result:</strong> I had been scoring the grounded arm against a stale baseline built on an older prompt. Rebuilt so both arms ran the same prompt, grounding fixed a domain call zero times and broke four across 162 classifications. It was retired outright, not left on as an option.</p>
        <p>An unfair baseline flatters the change you hoped for. The retrieval code stays in the repo so the measurement can be rerun. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html#decision-grounding">Full eval &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="decision-model" data-outcome="reversal">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Model gated on evidence<span class="code-ref">SYS-002 &middot; ADR-013</span></p>
        <h3>Gate model choice on evidence</h3>
        <p>Sonnet by default across the system. Escalate to Opus only where an eval shows the quality gain pays for itself. I built the upgrade, measured it, and declined it.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The result:</strong> routing the uncertain cases to the premium model moved <strong>zero rows</strong> on both axes at about 1.97&times; the cost per article. Sending everything to the premium model scored identically, so there was no headroom for any router to capture.</p>
        <p>A targeted prompt fix had already taken the ground routing was aimed at. The cheapest fix won. The harness stays in the repo as the record. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Full verdict &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="decision-eval">
        <p class="kicker">Eval rebuilt<span class="code-ref">EVAL &middot; v1 &rarr; v3</span></p>
        <h3>I rebuilt the eval on human-labeled real text</h3>
        <p>v1 graded the model on 300 snippets it generated itself. That measures consistency, not correctness. v2 replaced that with 54 hand-labeled real snippets, cross-checked by an Opus judge.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The result:</strong> domain accuracy fell from 97.3% to 88.9% on real text, and that lower number is the trustworthy one.</p>
        <p>Category accuracy actually rose, because real vocabulary separates more cleanly; domain fell because the synthetic set was trivially easy to grade. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Full eval &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <p class="ledger-intro">Eight more, in one line each. The full telling of every one is on the page it links to.</p>
      <ul class="ledger">
        <li id="decision-contracts">Decoupled the repos with frozen HTTP contracts &mdash; then audited the seam and found it <strong>broken with both repos' CI green</strong>, because each side asserted against its own copy of the shape. Two unit tests that happen to agree are not a contract test. <a href="projects/the-system.html">The System &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-falsegreen">Audited my own gates and found six of them reporting success for work that had not happened &mdash; a review lane that went green in <strong>36 seconds having posted nothing</strong>, and the counter built to catch that reading a key that does not exist. <a href="projects/false-green.html">False Green &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-hybrid" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Built hybrid lexical retrieval to rescue one specific miss, measured it, and kept the dense-only default &mdash; <strong>MRR identical to four decimals</strong>, and the miss it was built for turned out not to be a lexical failure at all. <a href="projects/kb-agent-retrieval.html">Retrieval &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-truncation">Nearly published a dramatic premium-vs-cheap model gap that was <strong>my own bug</strong> &mdash; a <code>max_tokens</code> cap truncated the cheap tier before its verdict on 20% of claims, and my scoring counted each as a disagreement. <a href="projects/faithfulness-judge.html">Judge &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-ceiling">Called it a ceiling, then went looking anyway &mdash; the misses clustered on one boundary, and two rubric clauses moved category <strong>90.7% &rarr; 94.4%</strong> with nothing regressing. A prompt problem wearing a ceiling's clothes. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-prompt" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>A prompt that read better to a human and measured worse &mdash; category <strong>79.0% &rarr; 76.7%</strong>, industry recall 0.217 &rarr; 0.100. Reverted. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-rightsized" data-outcome="reversal"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Built the enrichment loop on Kafka to learn it properly, then cut it &mdash; a single-user idempotent workload <strong>never needed a distributed log</strong>. Now an in-process background task. <a href="projects/the-system.html">The System &rarr;</a></li>
        <li id="decision-tooluse">Forced output to a tool schema instead of parsing prose, so an invalid label is rejected at the API layer &mdash; <strong>one out-of-enum response in 300</strong>, handled by a re-sample. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Classifier &rarr;</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="measure">
      <h2 class="section-title">Classifier evidence</h2>
      <p>Where the classifier stands now, on 54 hand-labeled real snippets.</p>
      <ul class="stat-strip">
        <li><span class="stat-value" data-metric="category_accuracy">94.4%</span><span class="stat-label">Category</span></li>
        <li><span class="stat-value" data-metric="domain_accuracy">98.1%</span><span class="stat-label">Domain</span></li>
        <li><span class="stat-value" data-metric="region_accuracy">94.4%</span><span class="stat-label">Region</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="stat-note">Category and region are scored separately. They happen to land on the same figure here.</p>
      <p>Those numbers are from the harder gold set. <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Full eval &rarr;</a></p>
      <p>The classical baseline that lost the case for an LLM is vendored here and runs in the browser, on the same rows. <a href="projects/classifier-baseline.html#try-it">Run it yourself &rarr;</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="measure">
      <h2 class="section-title">Field notes</h2>
      <p>Notes I keep while learning, in plain language, on the techniques behind all of this. <a href="https://sanlee-ys.github.io/learning-notes/">Read them &rarr;</a></p>
      <ul class="notes-list">
        <li>Structured output via tool-use</li>
        <li>Eval-driven development</li>
        <li>Reading the numbers</li>
        <li>Tool use, the general idea</li>
        <li>The agentic tool-use loop</li>
        <li>RAG: answering from your own docs</li>
        <li>Embeddings and vector stores</li>
        <li>Synthetic data and circular eval</li>
        <li>Tiered model routing</li>
        <li>Secrets and env</li>
        <li>Reproducible environments with uv</li>
        <li>Checkpoint, resume, and retries</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted-line">A selection; the full, growing set is on the notes site.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="measure about" id="about">
      <h2 class="section-title">About</h2>
      <p>
        For the short version of how I came to this work and how I approach it,
        read <a href="about.html">About &rarr;</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        It is a solo build, so the program layer is simulated &mdash; I lay out what
        that means on the <a href="projects/product-and-program.html">Product
        &amp; Program</a> page.
      </p>
      <p>
        A second record sits off this system: a physical lab that makes a
        factory-blank router provision itself.
        <a href="projects/netops-lab.html">Zero-touch provisioning &rarr;</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside the work I hike, take pictures, and live with a Scottish Fold
        named Sango. The rest is on
        <a href="about.html#outside">About</a> and in
        <a href="lab/gallery.html">the gallery</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="frame-strip" aria-label="A few frames from the gallery">
        <a class="frame" href="lab/gallery.html">
          <img src="assets/img/photo-pavilion-spring.jpeg" alt="A pavilion over a pond in spring bloom" loading="lazy" width="160" height="160" />
        </a>
        <a class="frame" href="lab/gallery.html">
          <img src="assets/img/photo-jeju.jpeg" alt="Basalt sea cliffs on Jeju Island" loading="lazy" width="160" height="160" />
        </a>
        <a class="frame" href="lab/gallery.html">
          <img src="assets/img/photo-lanterns.jpeg" alt="Lotus lanterns at a temple" loading="lazy" width="160" height="160" />
        </a>
        <a class="frame" href="lab/gallery.html">
          <img src="assets/img/photo-trail.jpeg" alt="A trail overlook in the mountains" loading="lazy" width="160" height="160" />
        </a>
      </div>
      <figure class="sango">
        <img src="assets/img/sango-boop.jpeg" alt="Sango, a Scottish Fold, asleep" loading="lazy" width="240" height="240" />
        <figcaption>Sango, Chief Nap Officer.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>
